    <section class="article__section article__abstract" aria-labelledby="abstract-title">
      <h2 id="abstract-title">Abstract</h2>
      <p>
        The present treatise develops a mathematically rigorous account of the relationship between
        the calculus and the perception of musical consonance and dissonance. The investigation
        proceeds from a single governing question: by what mathematical structures may the perceived
        ordering of musical intervals from consonant to dissonant be characterized, and to what extent
        does that ordering admit principled derivation from the apparatus of differential and integral
        calculus? The analysis is organized around four analytical instruments: the geometric sequence
        underlying twelve-tone equal temperament, the definite integral of the sinusoidal pressure
        waveform, the divergent series of integer-multiple harmonics that constitutes the spectrum of
        an idealized vibrating string, and the Fourier decomposition that connects time-domain
        waveforms to their spectral content.
      </p>
      <p>
        The treatment begins with a corrected formulation of the equal-tempered frequency relation,
        which is shown to be a geometric sequence rather than a geometric series, and traces the
        consequences of that distinction for the analytic description of pitch. It then examines the
        integral of one positive half-cycle of the sinusoidal pressure waveform, demonstrating that the
        ratio of such integrals between octave-related notes recovers, but does not exceed, the ordinary
        inverse relationship between frequency and wavelength. The harmonic series of overtones is then
        introduced, and its mathematical properties are examined as they bear upon the perceived sonority
        of a single note. Fourier analysis is then introduced as the canonical framework within which
        timbre is decomposed and within which the heuristic observations of music theory are placed into
        a quantitative setting.
      </p>
    </section>

